<robot name="partnet_1314c9a583812b9fc0eea8e38540939d">
    <!--
  INITIAL JOINT POSITIONS (For 'Close the drawer' task)
  ============================================================
  joint_0: 0.0
  joint_1: 0.2
  Reasoning: User requested 'first drawer fully closed' (joint_0) and 'second drawer slightly open' (joint_1). The images show both drawers at position 0.0m (closed). Joint_0 is set to 0.0m. Joint_1 is set to 0.2m, which is 20% of its 1.0m range, representing a 'slightly open' state for a drawer.
  ============================================================
  -->
    <!--
  Position/Orientation Stability.
  For this test, \mathbf{q}_0 denotes the initial joint configuration used to instantiate the articulated asset at reset.
  Starting from \mathbf{q}_0, we run the simulator for a settling period T_{set} to allow contacts and passive joints to reach a static equilibrium.
  We then record the root pose of the object as a reference state (\mathbf{x}_{ref}, \mathbf{R}_{ref}), and continue simulating for an additional horizon T_{test} with no external actuation (zero actions).
  Let (\mathbf{x}_t, \mathbf{R}_t) be the root position and orientation during this evaluation window.
  We define the stability deviations as the maximum drift from the reference:
    D_{pos}(\mathbf{q}_0) = max_{t in [0, T_{test}]} || \mathbf{x}_t - \mathbf{x}_{ref} ||_2,
    D_{ori}(\mathbf{q}_0) = max_{t in [0, T_{test}]} d(\mathbf{R}_t, \mathbf{R}_{ref}),
  where d(.,.) is the geodesic distance on SO(3).
  Passing thresholds: D_{pos} <= 0.001 m and D_{ori} <= 0.01 rad.
  Low values indicate that the asset remains numerically stable and does not exhibit spurious drift after initialization.
  -->
    <link name="base">
        <inertial>
            <mass value="0.0" />
            <inertia ixx="1e-06" iyy="1e-06" izz="1e-06" ixy="0.0" ixz="0.0" iyz="0.0" />
            <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="0 0 0" />
        </inertial>
    </link>
